Track 2: "Did the Mayflies Melt the Copper, Mister?"
- Story & Context: Shivering on the rim of the dead fountain basin, Hattie Vance used a rusted pair of poultry shears to strip the remaining braided copper from her runaway father’s 1958 Philco radio, tasting the bitter tang of lead solder on her cracked bottom lip. She spliced the salvaged wire directly into the monitor power supply of a water-damaged Centipede unit, coaxing out a thick, molasses-slow drone that mimicked the dying whine of summer riverbugs. The county sheriff's headlights swept across the boarded-up mall entrance, but Hattie refused to unplug the machine because the warm vibration against her chest was the only heat she had felt in weeks. Tucking her mouth directly to the speaker grille, she confessed her loneliness to the cavernous retail corridors, singing "I stripped my daddy's coils just to keep the winter warm," until the tape loop wore thin and dissolved into soft, woolly hiss.
