Track 3: Virgil Vance’s Caterpillar Church
- Story & Context: Folks down in McMinn County swear that when an August lightning bolt struck the mall’s glass pyramid, old Virgil Vance didn't perish—he simply molted into the building's electrical wiring. The strike permanently welded fifty daisy-chained arcade displays to his 1955 Fender pedal steel, leaving behind only two scorched work boots and a floor-shaking, warm B-flat drone that still makes the wild kudzu shaking inside the broken escalators bloom out of season. Runaway teenagers still climb through the service chutes just to sleep next to that vibrating steel frame, swearing the room-temperature drone cures mountain fever and soothes bad nerves. Whenever the summer heat causes the asphalt outside to bubble, the empty mall's intercom system reportedly kicks on by itself to broadcast Virgil's spectral drawl: "We shed our country skins beneath the dead mall dome."
